      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fort Hancock began as Camp Rice in 1882 along the San Antonio‑El Paso Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hancock%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Renamed in 1886 after Union general Winfield Scott Hancock, a Gettysburg hero.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hancock%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town sits on the U.S.–Mexico border opposite El Porvenir, Chihuahua.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hancock%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Fort Hancock–El Porvenir International Bridge links the two communities, and the Port of Entry lies on the Texas side.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hancock%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rail lines reached Fort Hancock by 1887, quickly making it a railroad stop before the post was abandoned.",
